{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"alkaline-lemon-copperhead","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# An inbound peer link-removal never converges when a pair holds two relations\n\n## Mechanism, established in source\n\n`rebar.unlink(id1, id2, *, repo_root=None)` takes **no `relation`** argument, and that is deliberate:\n`_commands/unlink.py`'s usage text says \"pair-scoped, NO relation arg; removes the most-recent link\nbetween the pair\", and `_get_link_info` returns \"the most recent net-active link for target\".\n\nBut links are STORED per relation: `graph/_links.add_dependency`'s docstring states it is idempotent\n\"if a net-active LINK with the same **(target_id, relation)** already exists\". So a pair CAN hold two\ndifferently-related deps (e.g. `blocks` and `relates_to`) simultaneously.\n\nThat asymmetry — write keyed on `(target, relation)`, remove keyed on `target` alone — is the defect.\n\n## The consequence, introduced knowingly by 2b16\n\nChange 1276 (bug `2b16-9be0-a8f5-41d9`) added the inbound link-removal path with guard **G5**: before\nunlinking, it confirms the pair's net-active relation MATCHES the relation whose peer link vanished, and\n**declines + logs** when it does not. That is the correct, safe direction — the alternative is removing a\nlink the peer still has, i.e. destroying local data.\n\nThe cost is that the decline is **permanent**: the removal is re-emitted and re-declined on every\nsubsequent pass, so the two systems never converge, and **there is no alert** — only a log line. This is\nthe \"silently non-convergent\" shape rather than the \"silently wrong\" shape, which is the better failure,\nbut it is still invisible.\n\n2b16's implementing agent flagged the API asymmetry but could not establish whether two relations can\nactually coexist for one pair; that question is settled above, so this is LIVE, not theoretical, and\nG5 is not dead code.\n\n## Options (deliberately not pre-decided)\n\n1. A relation-scoped removal — the honest fix, but it changes a public library signature\n   (`rebar.unlink`) or needs a lower-level seam, so it wants its own design pass.\n2. Keep the decline and make it OBSERVABLE — a bridge alert or a non-convergence counter, so an operator\n   sees a pair that will never settle instead of a log line nobody reads.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a test proves a pair CAN hold two differently-related net-active deps (or proves it cannot, which\n      would close this ticket as not_a_bug and make G5 dead code — establish which before designing)\n- [ ] a peer deletion of ONE relation in a two-relation pair reaches a defined end state: either the\n      correct link is removed, or the non-convergence is surfaced through an operator-visible channel\n      rather than a log line\n- [ ] whichever path is chosen, G5 never removes a link the peer still carries — assert that positively\n- [ ] mutation-checked, with verdicts recorded here","id":"e39f-5055-f5af-424a","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Inbound link removal never converges when a pair holds two relations (unlink is pair-scoped)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785728702924567001,"uuid":"37cf2791-d16c-48c0-bd9e-1a000e8ff0ef"}